Subject: [WASM research] rebase main 805925d8 native additions onto native31

Context-only research port of the following frozen mainline patches:
0065-xtensa-high-priority-irq-cooperative-yield.patch
  SHA256 f03b988372c17147f1b5f8c2fa2dc46366df362626d69b48febe45d48ab35560
0066-esp32-classic-rmt-legacy.patch
  SHA256 c88b1a85d7c00ae05aef995c3613a28684f011d0d13f0fbe845184136f4882d7

The native31 browser source omits optional S32C1I/handoff/IRQ diagnostic
context and contains a longer Unit list in hw/xtensa/meson.build. Re-anchor
the same added C logic at the matching include, exception-entry, helper and
return sites, and the RMT source next to m5_rmt.c. Diagnostic code is not
imported. This is not an assertion that native31 plus 0065/0066 applies
unmodified as 33 layers. Original patches and bootstrap remain unchanged.
Apply after the native31 and WASM 0001/0004/0005/0006/0009/0010 sequence;
0008 is optional. The explicit 64 KiB stack flags belong to the build.

The original icount_enabled guards remain: no forced cooperative yields
when icount is disabled. Added TCG helper calls may still have host cost.
The classic RMT data path is unchanged from 0066. No RF, electrical,
real-frequency or hardware-timing equivalence is established.


Native layer 0088 now owns the classic RMT source, call, declaration and
Meson wiring, including its real Unit GPIO/RMT route. This suffix retains
only the existing high-priority IRQ cooperative-yield changes.

--- a/accel/tcg/tcg-accel-ops-icount.c
+++ b/accel/tcg/tcg-accel-ops-icount.c
@@ -33,6 +33,34 @@
 #include "tcg-accel-ops.h"
 #include "tcg-accel-ops-icount.h"
 #include "tcg-accel-ops-rr.h"
+
+static unsigned icount_high_priority_irq_cpus;
+
+void icount_note_high_priority_irq(int cpu_index, bool active)
+{
+    unsigned mask = 1U << cpu_index;
+
+    if (!icount_enabled()) {
+        return;
+    }
+    if (active) {
+        icount_high_priority_irq_cpus |= mask;
+    } else {
+        icount_high_priority_irq_cpus &= ~mask;
+    }
+}
+
+void icount_yield_for_high_priority_irq(CPUState *cpu)
+{
+    if (icount_enabled() && icount_high_priority_irq_cpus) {
+        cpu_exit(cpu);
+    }
+}
+
+bool icount_high_priority_irq_active(void)
+{
+    return icount_enabled() && icount_high_priority_irq_cpus != 0;
+}
 
